{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MKMiYBdh8dMch7ETJnhRzQ","type":"tool_result","content":"1. **The blind -04 holdouts score 2 of 3.**\n   - The doctor and review rows hit.\n   - \"Dig into how hospitals de-identify patient records …, and spend eight iterations on it\" gets no recommendation: \"dig into\" is not read as a research cue.\n   - It is reported, not tuned, so the rows stay blind.\n2. **The argument hints are over the soft budget.** Both hints now list every offer flag (Q013), so `validate_document.py` warns that each is over 140 characters. The warning is soft, and no failure from a long hint is recorded.\n3. **\"Write a memo on\" is not read as writing about a topic.** \"write a memo on iterative research funding for the grant committee\" gets `/deep:research pol=ask`, and so does the same memo on \"deep research funding\", which was a loop ask before this packet. No guard is added for one phrasing.\n4. **The multi-round research alias also catches a memo about research funding.** The operator kept it for K2 (ADR-002), so that memo can surface the hub as a skill suggestion with no command.\n5. **The F6 session asks the offer only some of the time.** Three `claude -p` runs on \"extend the finished deep review by 3 iterations\" each stopped before spending, but none asked the offer with options A, B and C. The plan-mode run (`scratch/behaviour/F6-plan-mode.md`, $0.22) composed `/deep:review <packet> :auto --extend=3` and wrote a plan for approval. The run outside plan mode (`scratch/behaviour/F6.md`, $0.18) asked the spec-folder question and named `--extend=3` as the command it would run, after reading only the flag table. Step 0 of both command documents now names the three options, and the flag table sends a composed flag into option A. The run after that edit (`scratch/behaviour/F6-after-step0.md`, $0.16) asked which review was meant before it opened the command document, so the edit was never read. The session acts on the brief's `ask` alone, and the offer format lives one \n6. **A typed command on a stuck run.** \"my deep review run is stuck, /deep:review again\" gets `/doctor:deep-loop` from `attachCommand` and no run row from the Python gate, but the typed-command path still puts `/deep:review (ask)` on the head. The typed command is the reader's own choice, so it was left to outrank the diagnosis. The operator confirmed that choice on 2026-11-23.\n7. **Known misses of the word rules.** \"Skip the deep review on this branch\" is not read as a refusal, because \"skip\" also opens requests. \"multi-pass compiler design review\" offers the loop again, since three words may now stand before the loop's noun.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:limitations -->","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"<redacted>","uuid":"<redacted>","timestamp":"2026-11-24T04:51:29.473Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"1. **The blind -04 holdouts score 2 of 3.**\n   - The doctor and review rows hit.\n   - \"Dig into how hospitals de-identify patient records …, and spend eight iterations on it\" gets no recommendation: \"dig into\" is not read as a research cue.\n   - It is reported, not tuned, so the rows stay blind.\n2. **The argument hints are over the soft budget.** Both hints now list every offer flag (Q013), so `validate_document.py` warns that each is over 140 characters. The warning is soft, and no failure from a long hint is recorded.\n3. **\"Write a memo on\" is not read as writing about a topic.** \"write a memo on iterative research funding for the grant committee\" gets `/deep:research pol=ask`, and so does the same memo on \"deep research funding\", which was a loop ask before this packet. No guard is added for one phrasing.\n4. **The multi-round research alias also catches a memo about research funding.** The operator kept it for K2 (ADR-002), so that memo can surface the hub as a skill suggestion with no command.\n5. **The F6 session asks the offer only some of the time.** Three `claude -p` runs on \"extend the finished deep review by 3 iterations\" each stopped before spending, but none asked the offer with options A, B and C. The plan-mode run (`scratch/behaviour/F6-plan-mode.md`, $0.22) composed `/deep:review <packet> :auto --extend=3` and wrote a plan for approval. The run outside plan mode (`scratch/behaviour/F6.md`, $0.18) asked the spec-folder question and named `--extend=3` as the command it would run, after reading only the flag table. Step 0 of both command documents now names the three options, and the flag table sends a composed flag into option A. The run after that edit (`scratch/behaviour/F6-after-step0.md`, $0.16) asked which review was meant before it opened the command document, so the edit was never read. The session acts on the brief's `ask` alone, and the offer format lives one \n6. **A typed command on a stuck run.** \"my deep review run is stuck, /deep:review again\" gets `/doctor:deep-loop` from `attachCommand` and no run row from the Python gate, but the typed-command path still puts `/deep:review (ask)` on the head. The typed command is the reader's own choice, so it was left to outrank the diagnosis. The operator confirmed that choice on 2026-11-23.\n7. **Known misses of the word rules.** \"Skip the deep review on this branch\" is not read as a refusal, because \"skip\" also opens requests. \"multi-pass compiler design review\" offers the loop again, since three words may now stand before the loop's noun.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:limitations -->","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MKMiYBdh8dMch7ETJnhRzQ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
