-command-offers\n\nThe verdict is **PASS with advisories**: 0 P0, 0 P1 and 3 P2 findings over 5 of 5 iterations. Every dimension is covered, and the closing record says the run stopped at max iterations (`maxIterationsReached`).\n\n1. **A typed command on a stuck run (P2, was P1).** For \"my deep review run is stuck, /deep:review again\", the native advisor's brief offers `/deep:review (ask)`. That happens because the typed-command step in `advisor-recommend.ts` runs last and overrides the stuck-run diagnosis. The Python fallback gives no run row for the same prompt. I lowered it from P1 because the packet's implementation summary records that you accepted the typed command winning, on 2026-11-23. The gap left is that the packet's requirements and acceptance criteria (REQ-020, SC-005, AC-027) still say the two advisors agree, and the packet's own hook capture shows they don't.\n2. **Slow check on repeated commands (P2).** The check that reads how a command is named rescans the whole prompt for every time the command appears, so cost grows with the square of the repeats. The 10,000-character prompt cap bounds it: about 0.35 s extra in the Python fallback at the cap.\n3. **Partial drift guard (P2).** The test that keeps the TypeScript and Python gates in step only checks three exported word lists. Inline words such as the spelled count words (thirty, forty, fifty, dozen) have no fixture case, so editing one copy would pass both suites. A comparison of both gates on 61 new prompts showed no drift today.\n\nNothing else turned up: no regex backtracking problem, and a prompt cannot set the typed flag. The 013 typed head matches the injection contract and the changelog.\n\nThere is no built `dist` in this worktree, so I tested the TypeScript source through a small loader. I couldn't load the recommend handler that way. Finding 1 therefore rests on reading the code plus the packet's own hook capture, not on a run of my own.\n\nEvery write stayed inside the lineage director